1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,640
Now, 70s Tech on Modern Marbles. Can you dig it?

2
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:19,880
Big froze. Funky threads. Far out fads.

3
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:26,400
In the 1970s, Americans left conformity at the door and adopted a hedonistic lifestyle.

4
00:00:26,401 --> 00:00:31,400
The self-absorbed era became known as the me decade.

5
00:00:31,401 --> 00:00:34,400
And the breakthrough technologies that help power this attitude

6
00:00:34,401 --> 00:00:38,400
have matured into the way cool gadgetry we now take for granted.

7
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:43,400
Case in point, video and computer games.

8
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,400
Each year, hundreds of millions of dedicated gamers

9
00:00:49,401 --> 00:00:53,400
drop upwards of $20 billion of cold hard cash

10
00:00:53,401 --> 00:00:55,400
to feed what's become a national obsession.

11
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:07,400
But the monster success of this multi-billion dollar industry can trace its origin to a simple blip of an icon that's totally 70s.

12
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:13,400
Pong.

13
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:16,180
Don't miss the ball.

14
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:25,520
But before there was Pong, there was the video game Ping Pong, the brainchild of television engineer Ralph Baer.

15
00:01:25,521 --> 00:01:30,420
It all began in 1966.

16
00:01:30,421 --> 00:01:34,460
While employed by a defense contractor, Bayer came up with the idea of a device that would

17
00:01:34,461 --> 00:01:40,820
allow people to play games using their television sets.

18
00:01:40,821 --> 00:01:42,980
The motivation was very simple.

19
00:01:42,981 --> 00:01:46,760
Forty million TV sets are out there in the U.S. alone.

20
00:01:46,761 --> 00:01:52,020
If you attach something to one percent of those sets, that's 400,000 units.

21
00:01:52,021 --> 00:01:55,360
Multiply that by a sales price of 25 bucks.

22
00:01:55,361 --> 00:01:59,240
That's $100 million worth of stuff.

23
00:01:59,241 --> 00:02:04,420
Within two years, Behr and several associates completed construction of a prototype.

24
00:02:04,421 --> 00:02:09,520
They called it the Brown Box.

25
00:02:09,521 --> 00:02:15,760
Like the original Brown Box, this replica stores only 128 kilobytes of memory, equivalent

26
00:02:15,761 --> 00:02:19,760
to a one-page Microsoft Word document.

27
00:02:19,761 --> 00:02:23,340
But that's enough brain power to generate simple shapes.

28
00:02:23,341 --> 00:02:26,340
In 1968, using these simple shapes,

29
00:02:26,341 --> 00:02:29,220
Bayer's team came up with an idea destined to help create

30
00:02:29,221 --> 00:02:32,380
an entertainment renaissance.

31
00:02:32,381 --> 00:02:34,580
Let's have a machine control spot,

32
00:02:34,581 --> 00:02:38,460
a spot that's moved by some logic in the circuitry.

33
00:02:38,461 --> 00:02:41,160
The instant we had the spot moving back and forth,

34
00:02:41,161 --> 00:02:43,140
we knew we had something.

35
00:02:43,141 --> 00:02:46,900
And that something evolved into ping pong.

36
00:02:46,901 --> 00:02:49,540
This rare 1967 footage featuring Ralph

37
00:02:49,541 --> 00:02:52,000
bear is one of the earliest recordings of a video game.

38
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,440
Here we go. Up and down and up. He's getting tricky.

39
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:01,920
Missed it. One up.

40
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:08,080
A series of circuits controlled Ping Pong's gameplay.

41
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:11,600
When the ball appeared to come in contact with the paddle,

42
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:18,320
an electronic signal was sent to a logic circuit that triggered an output signal to a so-called flip-flop switch.

43
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:26,940
The signal caused the flip-flop to alter its state and change the direction of the ball.

44
00:03:26,941 --> 00:03:33,720
This phenomenon occurred every time the ball touched a paddle or a wall.

45
00:03:33,721 --> 00:03:38,280
Although groundbreaking, Bear's ping-pong lacked some fundamental gaming elements, such

46
00:03:38,281 --> 00:03:43,920
as sound and a score counter.

47
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:47,640
The racket didn't alter the trajectory of the ball.

48
00:03:47,641 --> 00:03:51,080
Instead, a player used an English knob on the controller,

49
00:03:51,081 --> 00:03:54,020
which is seen here dramatically change the direction of the ball

50
00:03:54,021 --> 00:03:55,320
after it left the racket.

51
00:03:55,321 --> 00:03:56,820
Down I go.

52
00:03:56,821 --> 00:03:58,920
Now watch me fake him up.

53
00:03:58,921 --> 00:04:01,160
Despite Ping Pong's limitations,

54
00:04:01,161 --> 00:04:05,620
Baer sensed in 1970 that he had a marketable game.

55
00:04:05,621 --> 00:04:10,960
He licensed his brown box to TV manufacturer Magnavox.

56
00:04:10,961 --> 00:04:13,640
Magnavox renamed it the Odyssey.

57
00:04:13,641 --> 00:04:15,400
They built about half a dozen boxes,

58
00:04:15,401 --> 00:04:18,320
They took them all over the country for consumer acceptance testing.

59
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:25,260
In September 1971, Magnavox tested the Odyssey in Burlingame, California.

60
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:33,120
A young engineer named Nolan Bushnell, who designed one of the first arcade games, Computer Space, was in attendance.

61
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:37,920
I'd been in the video game business for about two years at that time.

62
00:04:38,340 --> 00:04:44,640
And I'd heard about this video game coming to Burlingame, so I went up to take a look at it.

63
00:04:44,641 --> 00:04:47,360
And I thought it was a pretty dreadful game, actually.

64
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:52,520
Even so, Bushnell recognized its potential.

65
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:59,520
And the new company he founded, Atari, decided to create an arcade version of ping pong called Pong.

66
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:02,380
We turned it into a game.

67
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,980
The Odyssey really wasn't a game, you know, in a lot of ways.

68
00:05:06,981 --> 00:05:10,340
It wasn't a lot of strategy. There was no scoring, no sound.

69
00:05:10,341 --> 00:05:19,960
But the most important characteristic was the algorithm by which where the ball hit on the paddle, it created an angle.

70
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:26,840
So it allowed a paddle to be an offensive weapon rather than just blocking the ball.

71
00:05:28,780 --> 00:05:34,980
In 1972, priced just under $100, the Odyssey hit store shelves.

72
00:05:34,981 --> 00:05:42,860
Priced at a quarter per game, Atari's Pong debuted at a local tavern in Sunnyvale, California.

73
00:05:43,340 --> 00:05:46,000
It was in the bar for a day and a half or two days.

74
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:47,580
We get a service call.

75
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,740
The service call says, hey, get this machine out of here.

76
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:51,460
It doesn't work.

77
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:59,320
And we go down, open it up, and it had taken so many quarters that it totally jammed up the coin box.

78
00:05:59,321 --> 00:06:09,360
By 1974, when Atari's Pong was still gobbling up quarters, the original Magnavox Odyssey had ceased production.

79
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:18,900
Three years later, Bushnell invested the millions he made with Pong to develop a home video game console dubbed the Atari 2600.

80
00:06:18,901 --> 00:06:25,180
Featuring the now familiar eight-directional joystick with a single red button, the revolutionary

81
00:06:25,181 --> 00:06:30,140
console produced an offspring of the Mi decade known as the gamer.

82
00:06:30,141 --> 00:06:40,460
A multi-billion dollar industry was off and running.

83
00:06:40,461 --> 00:06:48,420
The key component that made the Atari 2600 sing was a tiny high-tech wonder born in the

84
00:06:48,421 --> 00:06:52,460
70s destined to transform technology.

85
00:06:52,461 --> 00:06:58,180
The microprocessor or simply the chip.

86
00:06:58,181 --> 00:07:02,940
The electronic ancestor to the chip was the bulky vacuum tube.

87
00:07:02,941 --> 00:07:07,260
It was replaced by the smaller transistor in the 1960s.

88
00:07:07,261 --> 00:07:12,260
By the 1970s, engineers were building transistors of microscopic size.

89
00:07:12,261 --> 00:07:17,760
A transistor can turn the flow of a current on or off, allowing it to process the binary

90
00:07:17,761 --> 00:07:20,880
language of a computer.

91
00:07:20,881 --> 00:07:25,980
By placing thousands of transistors onto a semiconductor, engineers generated the processing

92
00:07:25,981 --> 00:07:29,820
power of a large computer on a single chip.

93
00:07:29,821 --> 00:07:39,820
In 1971, Intel engineered the world's first microprocessor and designated it the Intel 4004.

94
00:07:39,821 --> 00:07:42,771
It was the first computer on a chip.

95
00:07:42,772 --> 00:07:49,820
In fact, when we advertised it, and that was November of 1971, it was not a modest ad.

96
00:07:49,821 --> 00:07:53,820
We said announcing a new era of integrated electronics.

97
00:07:53,821 --> 00:08:00,380
As chip integration got big, the size of electronics started getting small.

98
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:06,520
One of the first new gadgets produced by this giant leap towards miniaturization was the pocket calculator.

99
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:13,560
Leading the way in 1972 was Hewlett-Packard's HP35.

100
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:20,800
Its $395 price tag may have been a few too many numbers left of the decimal point for some,

101
00:08:20,801 --> 00:08:27,140
but it provided a lightning-fast alternative to every engineering geek's best friend, the slide rule.

102
00:08:28,020 --> 00:08:32,960
What I have here is a slide rule. This is what I used to get me through college.

103
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:36,880
What I have here is a TI-84 Plus Silver Edition.

104
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:43,680
Like the old HP-35, this modern calculator made by Texas Instruments

105
00:08:43,681 --> 00:08:47,740
has a speed limited only by how fast the operator can push its buttons.

106
00:08:47,741 --> 00:08:51,740
But exactly how much faster is it than a slide rule?

107
00:08:51,741 --> 00:08:54,740
Contestants, what's the square root of 23?

108
00:08:54,741 --> 00:08:58,740
Ben.

109
00:08:58,741 --> 00:09:02,740
It's going to be a while.

110
00:09:02,741 --> 00:09:08,740
You don't mind me singing while I'm doing this, do you?

111
00:09:08,741 --> 00:09:10,740
Yeah.

112
00:09:10,741 --> 00:09:12,740
Let's see.

113
00:09:12,741 --> 00:09:18,540
That's about 4.8.

114
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:21,560
4.79.

115
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:29,840
When you look at the calculator, it gave you incredible accuracy to the right of the decimal point

116
00:09:29,841 --> 00:09:32,100
that with a slide rule, you just can't get that.

117
00:09:32,101 --> 00:09:36,140
Although the microchip was integral in creating handheld consumer electronics,

118
00:09:36,620 --> 00:09:41,660
it required another 70s breakthrough to translate its binary language into a readable display.

119
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:45,540
The light-emitting diode, or LED.

120
00:09:47,220 --> 00:09:54,520
An LED is a semiconductor junction, and you pump in voltage and current, basically,

121
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:57,020
and that excites electrons in the material.

122
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:01,380
And then when the electrons drop back down to their normal state, they emit a photon.

123
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:03,100
The photon is light.

124
00:10:05,180 --> 00:10:09,600
Combining microchips and LEDs not only created portable electronics for work,

125
00:10:09,601 --> 00:10:15,300
like the pocket calculator, but also handheld toys for fun, like Mattel football.

126
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:25,560
And in a decade known for questionable style, LED watches became a fashion statement.

127
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:28,500
The concept of an LED watch is kind of odd.

128
00:10:28,580 --> 00:10:30,840
It's very difficult to read in the daylight, for example.

129
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:34,540
You need two hands to tell the time, so that's not too good in a watch.

130
00:10:34,541 --> 00:10:41,580
LED watches didn't survive the 70s, but the technology they harnessed is alive and well,

131
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:48,080
and lighting up a style of high-definition TVs known as DLP, or digital light projection.

132
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:53,920
This is a sample of red, green, and blue high-power devices.

133
00:10:54,060 --> 00:10:55,940
They're not fully on here, or I'd be blinded.

134
00:10:56,500 --> 00:11:01,580
But you can see that by turning the lights on and off, you can generate different colors.

135
00:11:01,581 --> 00:11:13,224
In a DLP TV, we turn these devices on and off in microseconds, and they shine on our device, and the mirrors flip on and off and generate exactly the right color for

136
00:11:13,225 --> 00:11:14,000
each pixel.

137
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:23,320
While LEDs had us seeing red in the 70s, one iconic gadget of the decade was filling our ears with sound.

138
00:11:23,321 --> 00:11:37,321
In 1978, Texas Instruments introduced millions of Americans to the first handheld electronic device with something to say.

139
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:44,280
The speak and spell.

140
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:49,400
While it may look like child's play, inside was state-of-the-art 70s tech.

141
00:11:49,401 --> 00:11:54,800
If you were to open it up, and we tried to make sure that children couldn't open it up,

142
00:11:55,280 --> 00:11:57,860
you would find a microprocessor, a TMS-1000.

143
00:11:58,860 --> 00:12:02,820
There were two memory chips that stored all of the speech data.

144
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:07,360
And then the fourth was the DSP, or the speech synthesizer.

145
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:12,560
And its purpose was to take the speech data out of the memory devices.

146
00:12:12,561 --> 00:12:20,480
A DSP, or digital signal processor, is a specialized microchip that can perform a multitude of

147
00:12:20,481 --> 00:12:24,120
complex mathematics in the same amount of time it takes to press a button.

148
00:12:24,121 --> 00:12:35,240
It was, in many views, including mine, the first digital signal processor that was ever created.

149
00:12:35,241 --> 00:12:42,320
When Speak and Spell hit the market in 1978, Texas Instruments couldn't produce them fast

150
00:12:42,321 --> 00:12:48,960
enough to keep up with demand. Kids loved it, partly because it was user-friendly in

151
00:12:48,961 --> 00:12:50,280
the truest sense possible.

152
00:12:50,281 --> 00:12:57,440
It felt more like a friend giving you your words than a teacher. It never raised its

153
00:12:57,441 --> 00:13:04,880
voice, it never yelled, it never screamed at you, and that made it a very nice environment

154
00:13:04,881 --> 00:13:05,880
for a child.

155
00:13:05,881 --> 00:13:08,880
For settings, press one.

156
00:13:08,881 --> 00:13:11,880
Today, DSP technology is pervasive,

157
00:13:11,881 --> 00:13:14,880
utilized in cell phones and car navigation systems.

158
00:13:14,881 --> 00:13:17,880
Make a U-turn, if possible.

159
00:13:17,881 --> 00:13:20,880
The refined speech of these gadgets is a product of advancements

160
00:13:20,881 --> 00:13:24,880
in the processing power of digital signal processors.

161
00:13:24,881 --> 00:13:27,880
So, the next time your electronic device starts wrapping,

162
00:13:27,881 --> 00:13:32,880
give props to the Speak & Spell, a way-cool gadget from the 70s.

163
00:13:32,881 --> 00:13:33,880
Say it.

164
00:13:33,881 --> 00:13:37,320
While Speak and Spell produced groundbreaking sounds,

165
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:41,760
another technological wonder of the decade was breaking the sound barrier.

166
00:13:42,980 --> 00:13:47,360
In the 1970s, commercial air travel was turning supersonic.

167
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:54,960
Atari derived its name from a Japanese term used in the game Go.

168
00:13:55,580 --> 00:14:00,460
It describes a scenario in which the opposing player's game piece will be captured on the next turn.

169
00:14:00,461 --> 00:14:04,460
70s tech will return on modern marvels

170
00:14:08,460 --> 00:14:18,700
its style regal its passengers elite its velocity twice the speed of sound

171
00:14:18,701 --> 00:14:27,940
in 1976 the concord ushered in the age of supersonic air travel with a ferocious sonic boom

172
00:14:27,941 --> 00:14:37,100
Although it flew for nearly 30 years, only 2.5 million travelers ever climbed aboard,

173
00:14:37,101 --> 00:14:42,520
a tiny fraction of the 54 million Americans that now fly each month.

174
00:14:42,521 --> 00:14:45,160
To be a passenger on the Concorde was luxurious.

175
00:14:45,161 --> 00:14:52,660
It was the individual attention that you received, the nice leather seats, the drinks, the Chateaubriand,

176
00:14:52,661 --> 00:14:56,060
you were just treated royally.

177
00:14:56,061 --> 00:14:59,863
the luxury was the unique thrill of supersonic travel.

178
00:14:59,864 --> 00:15:02,540
At maximum speed, the Concorde covered

179
00:15:02,541 --> 00:15:07,434
23 miles a minute, soaring faster than the rotation of the Earth.

180
00:15:07,435 --> 00:15:08,940
And when cruising at

181
00:15:08,941 --> 00:15:14,360
60,000 feet, safely above air turbulence, passengers claimed they could view the curvature

182
00:15:14,361 --> 00:15:21,820
of the Earth. This entire spectacle was available for as much as $6,000, one way.

183
00:15:21,821 --> 00:15:30,400
the story of Concorde begins in 1962 while the Americans and Russians were engaged in the space

184
00:15:30,401 --> 00:15:36,520
race Britain and France shocked the world when they agreed to combine resources in another

185
00:15:36,521 --> 00:15:42,580
contest for technological superiority the race to build the world's first supersonic airliner

186
00:15:43,780 --> 00:15:49,600
they named their consortium the Concorde there were plenty of supersonic airplanes but no attempt

187
00:15:49,601 --> 00:15:53,200
had been made to carry passengers comfortably, reliably,

188
00:15:53,201 --> 00:15:56,640
over long distances at supersonic speed.

189
00:15:56,641 --> 00:15:59,940
Britain and France saw in the supersonic transport

190
00:15:59,941 --> 00:16:02,800
an opportunity to make one of these technological leaps

191
00:16:02,801 --> 00:16:05,840
that would focus attention upon themselves

192
00:16:05,841 --> 00:16:07,540
and surround them with glory.

193
00:16:10,300 --> 00:16:11,980
The proposed Concorde was designed

194
00:16:11,981 --> 00:16:17,160
to reach a maximum speed of 1,350 miles per hour,

195
00:16:17,161 --> 00:16:24,420
seat 100 passengers, and fly from Paris or London to New York City, nonstop, in just over

196
00:16:24,421 --> 00:16:27,780
three hours, less than half the time of a 707.

197
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:34,860
The intrepid move by Britain and France compelled both the United States and the Soviet Union

198
00:16:34,861 --> 00:16:36,120
to take action.

199
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:43,920
Once the ball started rolling, Russia quickly got involved, and shortly after that, the

200
00:16:43,921 --> 00:16:47,920
United States decided that if all these other people were building super-siding

201
00:16:47,921 --> 00:16:51,860
transports we certainly couldn't get left behind. Kennedy in a speech to the

202
00:16:51,861 --> 00:16:57,600
Air Force Academy said we must go there, we must be bigger, we must be better, we

203
00:16:57,601 --> 00:17:05,240
must be faster, and we must do it by the end of the 1960s. By 1965 a government

204
00:17:05,241 --> 00:17:09,780
plan was in place to allocate funds for 75% of the development cost for an

205
00:17:09,781 --> 00:17:15,717
American SST. The remaining tab would be paid by the company awarded the contract.

206
00:17:15,718 --> 00:17:17,600
That honor went to Boeing.

207
00:17:18,600 --> 00:17:30,286
Seemingly in response to some sort of patriotic notion that if ours wasn't way bigger, way better, faster, heavier, and in every way more glorious than theirs, we

208
00:17:30,287 --> 00:17:35,480
The American program was a kind of psychological freak on its own terms.

209
00:17:35,481 --> 00:17:37,500
would somehow be humiliated.

210
00:17:37,501 --> 00:17:42,860
the United States set out to build an airplane of almost unimaginable ambition.

211
00:17:43,820 --> 00:17:47,700
The proposed Boeing 2707 was a giant.

212
00:17:48,500 --> 00:17:53,260
Its fuselage would span 300 feet in length and seat 300 passengers.

213
00:17:54,140 --> 00:18:00,000
At maximum speed, it would travel at Mach 3, or 2,100 miles per hour.

214
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:03,460
Compared to its European rival, the Concorde,

215
00:18:03,461 --> 00:18:06,240
the Boeing aircraft would be 100 feet longer,

216
00:18:06,241 --> 00:18:11,540
hold three times as many passengers, and travel 60% faster.

217
00:18:14,380 --> 00:18:17,200
But instead of ripping through the skies at Mach 3,

218
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:23,580
the Boeing 2707 is sitting still at the Hiller Aviation Museum in San Carlos, California.

219
00:18:25,360 --> 00:18:28,240
This mock-up, built by Boeing in 1969,

220
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:32,580
is the closest the United States ever came to building an SST.

221
00:18:32,581 --> 00:18:36,880
We're in the fuselage of the Boeing 2707-300.

222
00:18:36,881 --> 00:18:40,320
This is actually the first wide-bodied aircraft ever

223
00:18:40,321 --> 00:18:41,021
designed.

224
00:18:41,022 --> 00:18:44,660
First class section on the Boeing 2707 is going to be 30,

225
00:18:44,661 --> 00:18:48,060
with about 270 people for the rear.

226
00:18:48,061 --> 00:18:50,560
But the inordinate cost of such an ambitious plane

227
00:18:50,561 --> 00:18:53,340
made it an economic loser.

228
00:18:53,341 --> 00:18:56,100
By 1971, the federal government voted

229
00:18:56,101 --> 00:18:59,580
to withdraw funding, ending the program.

230
00:18:59,581 --> 00:19:02,240
