473
00:34:52,981 --> 00:34:57,620
stringent government emission standards pressured Detroit to swap horsepower for

474
00:34:57,621 --> 00:35:05,360
improved gas mileage. Pontiac however bucked the trend releasing the Trans Am

475
00:35:05,361 --> 00:35:14,320
455 super duty in 1973 the most powerful muscle car of the decade and everybody pretty much said

476
00:35:14,321 --> 00:35:21,520
where in the heck did this thing come from it was a muscle car and more they were faster

477
00:35:21,521 --> 00:35:30,720
stronger than they had a need to be they were far and away from 1973 through the rest of 1970s

478
00:35:31,440 --> 00:35:32,780
They were the muscle car.

479
00:35:33,060 --> 00:35:34,360
Nothing else came close.

480
00:35:36,060 --> 00:35:40,440
The 1973 model was the first to feature the Firebird emblem on the hood.

481
00:35:42,240 --> 00:35:43,660
Although it was an eye-catcher,

482
00:35:44,420 --> 00:35:47,920
it's what was under the hood of the Super Duty that blew away the competition.

483
00:35:49,060 --> 00:35:51,440
The Super Duty engine was hand-assembled,

484
00:35:51,960 --> 00:35:53,780
a high-nickel block for strength.

485
00:35:54,260 --> 00:35:57,380
The cylinder heads were high-flow, big carburetor.

486
00:35:57,381 --> 00:35:59,860
May as well have been a funnel on the hood saying, gas here.

487
00:35:59,861 --> 00:36:04,860
Pontiac rated the Super Duty engine at 290 horsepower.

488
00:36:05,520 --> 00:36:09,380
Well, you know, it might have made 290 horsepower at about 1,500 RPM,

489
00:36:09,880 --> 00:36:13,940
but when you get in the power band, you were looking at well over 400 horsepower.

490
00:36:14,280 --> 00:36:16,500
These were serious drag strip monsters.

491
00:36:18,620 --> 00:36:21,240
Sure, this guzzler only got 10 miles to the gallon,

492
00:36:21,820 --> 00:36:25,180
but its sticker price of $3,000 didn't break the bank.

493
00:36:26,640 --> 00:36:29,060
When commandeered by a driver with a lead foot,

494
00:36:29,061 --> 00:36:33,640
the Super Duty could rocket from zero to 100 in just 10 seconds.

495
00:36:37,060 --> 00:36:39,680
Driving a Trans Am was definitely a gas,

496
00:36:40,200 --> 00:36:44,500
but it was a shortage of that valuable resource that essentially killed the Super Duty.

497
00:36:45,440 --> 00:36:50,320
In 1973, a national energy crisis forced gas prices to double.

498
00:36:51,260 --> 00:36:55,120
Gas-thirsty muscle cars like the Super Duty were headed for the chopping block.

499
00:36:55,121 --> 00:37:04,300
Muscle cars were supplanted by fuel-efficient subcompact cars like the Gremlin, the Pacer, and the Pinto.

500
00:37:05,500 --> 00:37:09,320
Its unprotected fuel tank made it a hot car for a different reason.

501
00:37:16,660 --> 00:37:21,300
In 1975, Trans Am horsepower dropped to 200,

502
00:37:21,301 --> 00:37:24,600
but its reputation for performance and unique style

503
00:37:24,601 --> 00:37:26,880
continued to generate mondo sales.

504
00:37:28,080 --> 00:37:32,380
Then, in 1977, the popularity of the screaming chicken

505
00:37:32,381 --> 00:37:34,420
would reach movie star status.

506
00:37:35,380 --> 00:37:37,140
When Smokey and the Bandit hit,

507
00:37:37,360 --> 00:37:40,200
Trans Am production, almost overnight, had to double.

508
00:37:40,800 --> 00:37:42,720
The public was enamored with the car.

509
00:37:42,980 --> 00:37:45,180
They wanted the car. They wanted to be seen in the car.

510
00:37:45,360 --> 00:37:48,400
I think the term could just be described as explosive.

511
00:37:49,260 --> 00:37:50,580
Sales went through the roof.

512
00:37:51,300 --> 00:37:57,020
In 77 and 78, Pontiac sold over 150,000 Trans Ams,

513
00:37:57,400 --> 00:38:00,040
nearly twice the number sold in the previous six years,

514
00:38:00,680 --> 00:38:03,940
including thousands of the black and gold version made famous in the movie.

515
00:38:06,820 --> 00:38:11,780
Today, the Trans Am banded as a collector's item, worth as much as $40,000.

516
00:38:14,180 --> 00:38:18,160
I think a lot of people saw that movie and they fell in love with the car,

517
00:38:18,161 --> 00:38:19,380
maybe for the first time.

518
00:38:19,700 --> 00:38:21,120
I was a little kid when it came out.

519
00:38:21,300 --> 00:38:23,580
and it had a strong impact for me.

520
00:38:24,080 --> 00:38:26,100
I mean, this was the car I've always wanted to own.

521
00:38:28,120 --> 00:38:30,340
Although the Trans Am is closely identified

522
00:38:30,341 --> 00:38:32,440
with the style and attitude of the 70s,

523
00:38:32,720 --> 00:38:35,600
the model continued to sell up until 2002.

524
00:38:37,940 --> 00:38:39,840
Today, rumors rumble that Pontiac

525
00:38:39,841 --> 00:38:41,540
may be planning a Trans Am sequel,

526
00:38:42,160 --> 00:38:45,540
but perhaps minus the car's foul figurehead.

527
00:38:46,340 --> 00:38:48,600
If the Trans Am is reborn,

528
00:38:48,601 --> 00:38:52,100
I don't know if they're going to put a screaming chicken on the hood.

529
00:38:52,101 --> 00:38:56,100
The Firebird emblem that we know and love was born in the 70s.

530
00:38:56,101 --> 00:38:57,600
It was embraced by the 70s.

531
00:38:57,601 --> 00:38:59,600
It made a statement about the 70s.

532
00:38:59,601 --> 00:39:02,600
Maybe it just belongs in the 70s.

533
00:39:04,600 --> 00:39:08,600
In the 1970s, Trans Am owners weren't the only motorists screaming,

534
00:39:08,601 --> 00:39:10,600
I can't drive 55.

535
00:39:10,601 --> 00:39:14,100
Truckers were also pushing the needle above the double nickel.

536
00:39:14,101 --> 00:39:18,100
And a gadget in the cab kept them one step ahead of the law.

537
00:39:18,101 --> 00:39:23,240
Southbound, there's a Smokey sitting in that northbound scale house watching you as you go by.

538
00:39:23,940 --> 00:39:25,520
That's a 10-4, good buddy.

539
00:39:27,960 --> 00:39:30,740
According to Smokey and the Bandit director Hal Needham,

540
00:39:31,060 --> 00:39:37,740
one of two 1977 Trans Ams provided by Pontiac was destroyed during a stunt as it jumped a river.

541
00:39:38,440 --> 00:39:40,640
70s Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

542
00:39:42,740 --> 00:39:46,740
X-T-R-A, that is right.

543
00:39:46,741 --> 00:39:50,720
Some technologies from the 1970s have stood the test of time,

544
00:39:51,580 --> 00:39:54,140
evolving into gadgets we now can't live without.

545
00:39:54,480 --> 00:39:56,420
Make a U-turn, if possible.

546
00:39:58,100 --> 00:40:01,360
Others had a shelf life as brief as the streaking craze.

547
00:40:02,840 --> 00:40:04,240
Remember the leisure suit?

548
00:40:04,660 --> 00:40:05,760
The pet rock?

549
00:40:06,420 --> 00:40:07,700
And mood rings?

550
00:40:08,920 --> 00:40:11,560
Rare is the gadget that transcends its medium

551
00:40:11,561 --> 00:40:13,360
and becomes a national obsession.

552
00:40:13,361 --> 00:40:17,500
But that's what happened in the 1970s with the Citizens Band

553
00:40:17,600 --> 00:40:18,400
radio.

554
00:40:18,500 --> 00:40:23,300
In a decade in which few had car phones,

555
00:40:23,400 --> 00:40:25,800
the CB became the official mouthpiece

556
00:40:25,900 --> 00:40:28,700
for the American motorist.

557
00:40:28,800 --> 00:40:30,880
Grabbing a CB radio was the equivalent

558
00:40:30,980 --> 00:40:33,620
to shooting the breeze in an internet chat room.

559
00:40:33,720 --> 00:40:37,420
Drivers had their own nicknames or handles.

560
00:40:37,520 --> 00:40:41,420
Well, mine was Big Bob, and then there was Nasty, Smelly.

561
00:40:41,421 --> 00:40:47,500
smelly you know there's even stinky and spoke their own unique slanguage

562
00:40:49,020 --> 00:40:52,780
how about over our southbound what's going on at the chicken house up our new tucker

563
00:40:55,980 --> 00:41:01,180
copy that good buddy today's truckers mimic the slang used by their 70s brethren

564
00:41:02,380 --> 00:41:09,100
but the cb airwaves aren't quite as clogged in the 1970s americans snapped up an average of

565
00:41:09,101 --> 00:41:13,340
seven million CB radios a year.

566
00:41:13,341 --> 00:41:15,100
In those days, everybody went down the highway

567
00:41:15,101 --> 00:41:17,340
with one of those flagships flying around.

568
00:41:17,341 --> 00:41:18,700
They were talking to everybody.

569
00:41:18,701 --> 00:41:19,820
They were talking to Grandma.

570
00:41:19,821 --> 00:41:23,820
I had my grandmother on a CB, you know?

571
00:41:23,821 --> 00:41:27,580
A CB radio operates like any other radio transmitter.

572
00:41:27,581 --> 00:41:31,340
It has 40 channels and broadcasts over a narrow bandwidth,

573
00:41:31,341 --> 00:41:35,260
between 24 and 27 megahertz.

574
00:41:35,261 --> 00:41:37,740
But unlike the transmitter of a radio station,

575
00:41:37,741 --> 00:41:40,000
which broadcasts from a tall antenna tower,

576
00:41:40,580 --> 00:41:42,360
a CB radio has a short antenna,

577
00:41:43,020 --> 00:41:44,940
typically measuring only 10 feet tall.

578
00:41:46,200 --> 00:41:49,280
This limits its broadcast range to about 5 miles.

579
00:41:52,520 --> 00:41:55,620
In 1974, when the interstate speed limit

580
00:41:55,621 --> 00:41:58,320
was lowered from 70 to 55 miles per hour,

581
00:41:58,960 --> 00:42:01,060
the CB radio became the secret weapon

582
00:42:01,061 --> 00:42:02,600
for clock-conscious truckers.

583
00:42:03,640 --> 00:42:06,320
The truckers were interested in going

584
00:42:06,321 --> 00:42:12,360
more than 55 miles an hour, so they were looking for the CB to spot radar traps, and they were

585
00:42:12,361 --> 00:42:16,380
communicating between each other where the radar speed traps were.

586
00:42:18,380 --> 00:42:24,960
By 1975, pop culture had embraced CB radios, inspiring the four-wheeling motorists to jump

587
00:42:24,961 --> 00:42:26,580
on board the CB bandwagon.

588
00:42:29,680 --> 00:42:35,240
CB radio companies like Midland in Kansas City, Missouri, started churning out sets faster

589
00:42:35,241 --> 00:42:36,880
that a trucker could lay the hammer down.

590
00:42:42,720 --> 00:42:47,920
This particular CB model was one of our early 23-channel CB radios

591
00:42:47,921 --> 00:42:51,380
and was targeted to the four-wheel driver.

592
00:42:51,381 --> 00:42:55,820
It would be common to find it in Panto, Chevette, Gremlin, car like that.

593
00:42:56,680 --> 00:42:59,740
Early 70s retail price on this was around $100.

594
00:42:59,741 --> 00:43:06,780
As with most fads, the CB phenomenon gradually bugged out.

595
00:43:07,600 --> 00:43:11,340
By the 1980s, four-wheelers swapped their CB radios for cell phones,

596
00:43:11,920 --> 00:43:16,100
and the CB radio returned to its rightful owner, the trucker.

597
00:43:17,260 --> 00:43:18,580
That's a Big Ten 4.

598
00:43:21,440 --> 00:43:26,640
The far-out technology of the 70s reached far enough out to leave an enduring legacy.

599
00:43:26,641 --> 00:43:31,640
Yesterday's 70s toy is today's talking computer.

600
00:43:31,641 --> 00:43:34,640
In a quarter mile, make a left turn.

601
00:43:34,641 --> 00:43:37,640
The technology in that 70s handheld game

602
00:43:37,641 --> 00:43:40,640
now lights up your big screen TV.

603
00:43:40,641 --> 00:43:44,640
The iconic gadgets of the 70s may seem simple,

604
00:43:44,641 --> 00:43:47,640
but they've spawned a complex assortment of technologies

605
00:43:47,641 --> 00:43:49,640
that continue to enrich our lives.

